      "Language": "jpn",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND AND AIMS",
          "content": "Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) is a well-known important cause of non-cardiac chest pain (NCCP). It has been shown that in western countries this condition can be in 22-66% of the cases. However, this situation is unclear in Japanese hospitals. We performed this study to clarify the frequency of GERD in NCCP patients who attended the emergency room."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We investigated the final diagnosis of the patients with chest pain who attended the emergency room of our hospital in March, June, September and December of 2005."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Forty patients with NCCP attended the emergency room in the study periods. In these patients, only one patient (2.5%) was diagnosed as GERD."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The results of this study showed that the frequency of GERD in NCCP patients of emergency room of Japan is lower than that of western countries."
        }
      ]},
